# XSH readdir() and readdir_r()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.readdir.001
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For a directory holding files named "plain", "x.y.z", "-dash_", "A9"
           and ".hidden", readdir() returns each name once, byte for byte,
           and no other entry but dot and dot-dot (at most once each).
test       t/001-names.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.002
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       For a directory holding a file f, a directory s and a symbolic link
           sl to f, each entry's d_ino equals st_ino from lstat() of that
           name.
test       t/002-d-ino.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.003
anchor     functions/readdir.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       At the end of the stream readdir() returns a null pointer and leaves
           errno as it was (EDOM, set before every call), and does so again
           on two further calls.
test       t/003-end-errno.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.004
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The entry readdir() returned for one stream still holds the same
           name after another stream, of a different directory and then of
           the same directory, is read to the end.
test       t/004-streams-independent.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.005
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With the directory's st_atim set to 2001-09-09 (older than its
           st_mtim), reading it to the end with readdir() and closing the
           stream leaves st_atim later than that.
note       A file system mounted to skip directory access times
           (nodiratime) gives FAIL here; one that skips all access times
           (noatime) is detected in setup and gives UNRESOLVED.
test       t/005-atime.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.006
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       With an entry buffer of exactly offsetof(struct dirent, d_name) +
           {NAME_MAX} + 1 bytes followed by a guard, readdir_r(d, ent, &res)
           returns 0 with res == ent for each of a, b and a name of
           {NAME_MAX} bytes (and dot and dot-dot), then 0 with res a null
           pointer; the guard is untouched.
note       readdir_r() is obsolescent (OB) but still required.
test       t/006-readdir-r.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.007
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       For files named "with space", "tab\tname" and the bytes 0xc3 0xa9,
           readdir() returns each name once, byte for byte.
test       t/007-bytes.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.008
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       For a directory of 3000 files with 30-byte names, readdir() returns
           each name exactly once and no other entry but dot and dot-dot.
timeout    120
test       t/008-large.c

assertion  XSH.readdir.009
anchor     functions/readdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With a stream of a, b, c, d and e of which the parent read two
           entries (dot and dot-dot aside), a child that alone continues
           gets exactly the other three.
test       t/009-after-fork.c
